<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/21st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">United Nations Mission in Liberia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2003">2003</a>–<a href="/years/2018">2018</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a> · Also known as UNMIL</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> Mission in Liberia (UNMIL) was a peacekeeping operation established by the UN Security Council in September 2003, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a> Comprehensive Peace Agreement ended the Second Liberian Civil War. At its height it deployed some 15,000 troops, disarmed former combatants, supported the elections of 2005 and helped rebuild the security forces. It completed its mandate in March 2018.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The two wars killed an estimated 250,000 people and displaced over a million, and a United Nations mission, <b>UNMIL</b>, kept the peace until 2018.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
